    {
      "kind": "explanation",
      "title": "The stop is left out",
      "text": "Look at Hannah's loop line: `for n in range(1, 5)`. Python starts at 1 and stops before 5, so it prints 1, 2, 3, 4. This is **range(start, stop)**: the stop is left out. To get 5, write `range(1, 6)`. The count of numbers is stop minus start, so 6 − 1 = 5. Like a shop that closes at 5 p.m.: you can shop at 4:59, but not at 5.",
      "exampleLineIdea": null,
      "image": {
        "prompt": "The numbers 1 to 6 in a row on a horizontal line, each in a circle. Circles 1, 2, 3 and 4 are filled with bright color and joined by a bracket labeled range(1, 5). Circles 5 and 6 are gray and empty. Plain light background.",
        "alt": "The numbers 1 to 6 on a line; 1 to 4 are highlighted as range(1, 5), while 5 and 6 are gray and left out."
      },
      "visual": null,
      "explore": null
    },
    {
      "kind": "check",
      "context": "Hannah tries a new loop: `for n in range(2, 6): print(n)`. Each number prints on its own line.",
      "question": "Which numbers does it print?",
      "options": [
        {
          "text": "2, 3, 4, 5",
          "isCorrect": true,
          "reason": "It starts at 2 and stops before 6, so 5 is the last number printed.",
          "picture": null
        },
        {
          "text": "2, 3, 4, 5, 6",
          "isCorrect": false,
          "reason": "It's tempting to read the stop as the last number, but the stop is left out, so 6 never prints.",
          "picture": null
        },
        {
          "text": "3, 4, 5, 6",
          "isCorrect": false,
          "reason": "The start is included, so 2 does print. Only the stop is left out.",
          "picture": null
        },
        {
          "text": "3, 4, 5",
          "isCorrect": false,
          "reason": "This leaves out both ends. Only the stop is left out; the start, 2, still prints.",
          "picture": null
        }
      ],
      "image": null,
      "visual": null,
      "basis": {
        "screen": 2,
        "quote": "Python starts at 1 and stops before 5, so it prints 1, 2, 3, 4."
      }
    },
    {
      "kind": "activity",
      "template": "codeRunner",
      "content": "{\"prompt\":\"Fix the loop so it prints the numbers 1 to 5, one per line.\",\"fields\":{\"language\":\"python\",\"fileName\":\"counter.py\",\"starterCode\":\"for n in range(1, 5):\\n    print(n)\",\"editableLines\":[1],\"solution\":\"for n in range(1, 6):\\n    print(n)\",\"expectedOutput\":\"1\\n2\\n3\\n4\\n5\",\"mistakes\":[{\"output\":\"1\\n2\\n3\\n4\",\"feedback\":\"Still stops at 4. The stop is left out, so to print 5 the stop has to be 6.\"},{\"output\":\"0\\n1\\n2\\n3\\n4\",\"feedback\":\"Now it starts at 0 and still misses 5. Keep the start at 1 and raise the stop.\"},{\"output\":\"1\\n2\\n3\\n4\\n5\\n6\",\"feedback\":\"The stop went one too far. Stop at 6 to end on 5, not 7.\"}]},\"check\":{\"kind\":\"interaction\",\"explanation\":\"range(1, 6) starts at 1 and stops before 6, so it prints 1, 2, 3, 4 and 5. The stop is always one more than the last number you want.\"}}"
    },
    {
      "kind": "check",
      "context": "Leah builds a New Year's Eve countdown screen for a party in Times Square, New York. Her loop is `for n in range(10, 0, -1): print(n)`. The -1 means count down one at a time.",
      "question": "What is the last number it prints?",
      "options": [
        {
          "text": "1",
          "isCorrect": true,
          "reason": "The stop, 0, is still left out when counting down, so the loop ends at 1.",
          "picture": null
        },
        {
          "text": "0",
